WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.803
4.35 million.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.905
9.44 million.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:09.109
83%. What do those numbers have to do with data security?

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.011
I'll tell you in a minute.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:15.348
Welcome back to the Cyber Security Architecture series,
where in the first few videos

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:20.053
we talked about principles, fundamentals,
about the various domains of cybersecurity.

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.654
We covered identity and access.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:25.859
We looked at the endpoint,
the network, applications in the last video.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:30.230
Today, we're going to talk about data security,
which is what these numbers have to do with.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.699
So the first one, 4.35 million.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:37.704
Well, according to the Ponemon Institute,
that's the average cost of a data

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.739
breach worldwide.

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:43.877
That's a lot of money every time somebody breaks in,

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.980
that's what it's costing the organization on average.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.284
And by the way, that's taking out some of the really
large numbers that might have skewed the average.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.820
So this is the baseline that we're looking at,

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:58.258
9.44 million--cost of a data breach

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.428
in the US. Twice as much,

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:06.866
if you're in the US, if you get hit with this. And 83%,
what does that have to do with anything?

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:11.771
That is the number of organizations
that have been hit by more than one data breach.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:16.709
So this is answering the question
"Why do we need to care about data security?"

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:21.781
Now we're going to talk about in the rest of the video
about some of these aspects,

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:26.553
about governance, about discovery,
about protection, compliance, detection, response.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:27.921
Hang around to the end.

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:31.257
And I'm going to tell you the top
five things that this survey found

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:34.494
will reduce the cost of a data breach for you.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.830
So stay tuned for that.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.566
First of all, we're going to cover governance and discovery.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.069
So think of this as a data security ecosystem.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.671
We're going to fill this thing out.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:48.741
These are the technologies that go into allowing us
to secure our data.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:53.179
First of all, we have to have some sort of governance plan.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.583
This is our way of saying this is what we want to do.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:00.553
If I don't define where the finish line
is, I can expect people to end up there.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.592
So first of all, we're going to have a policy,
a data security policy, and in that,

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:09.095
we're going to spell out what kinds of things are sensitive
and what kinds of things aren't.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.998
We're going to spell out a classification criteria

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:16.169
that says this is keys to the kingdom
and this is the lunchroom menu.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:17.637
Nobody needs to protect that.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.606
We don't really care, that sort of thing.

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.608
We need to spell out that criteria.

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:26.779
We need to also spell out what kinds of protections
go with those different levels of sensitivity.

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:29.849
So we may have confidential, unclassified.

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:33.086
We may have super confidential, super top secret, super

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:36.122
duper top secret, whatever your classification scheme is.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.391
But you need to spell out what those are.

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:41.261
We can't expect the users in our company

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.898
to protect the data
if we haven't spelled out what those guidelines are.

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:48.134
So it all starts with this governance policy classification.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:52.205
Build a catalog that tells us this is where the data is.

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:56.943
This is where the really sensitive stuff is,
and you want to know where that is all the time.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.212
We're going to be updating that as well.

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:05.151
And then build a resilience plan that says if we lose data,
how do we recover?

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.654
So we'll talk a little more about that later as well.

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:12.292
So that's the first stage is build the plan,
then we're going to move into discovery.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:17.997
Now, I've said
this is what I want to do to cover our data and protect it.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:20.700
Now let's go find where the data is.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.605
A couple of different places
that we can look for data types--databases.

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:29.876
That's where we expect
that's going to be structured data in most cases.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:32.579
That's we're going to see a lot of the keys to the kingdom.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:35.081
But don't overlook this other area.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.817
Files, emails, spreadsheets,

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.987
all kinds of other things that make up unstructured data.

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:45.925
We need to be able to discover sensitive content
in unstructured data as well.

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:49.329
In other words, in places where we might not otherwise look.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:53.733
We expect the keys to the kingdom
to be in the very sensitive database.

00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:56.069
But what if somebody makes a copy of that?

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.606
What if somebody excerpts parts of it
and emails it to someone?

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.842
Now we've got sensitive data
flying around a lot of other places,

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:08.615
and I'd like to look on the network
and see as this data is moving around as well,

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:13.353
and maybe discover when there are certain types of issues
that we could be running into.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.390
And one of those discovery things will help us in particular

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.326
with a thing called data loss protection.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:25.732
So DLP is a technology
that's important that allows us to discover this stuff

00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:30.870
in real time on various systems
and as data is flowing across our networks.

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:32.672
Okay.

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:35.108
Now, we've covered the why of data security.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:38.011
These numbers down here
should be sufficient motivation for you.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:42.181
How do we do governance and discovery,
some of the general technologies there.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.184
Now, let's talk about protection and compliance.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:51.624
I figured out how I need to secure things
and where the stuff is. Now

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.793
how am I going to actually protect it?

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:56.162
Well, encryption is a huge part of that.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:05:01.067
That is, I scramble the data
so that only I can read it and the bad guys can't.

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:03.703
And I need to be able to do that for data at rest.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:07.540
Like sitting in a database, for instance, or data at motion.

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:11.611
Data that's in motion
could be moving from one user to another,

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:15.715
could be on a web server,
and then being served up over across the Internet.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:20.453
So there's I wouldn't need to be able to keep the data secure
all the time at rest

00:05:20.453 --> 00:05:23.523
and in motion if I'm going to be encrypting data.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:27.927
The other thing I need to be able to do a really good job of
is managing the keys.

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:30.296
If you lose the keys, you lose the data.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:34.467
So remember that we want to generate keys in a random way.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.271
If anyone can predict the way keys are generated,
then all bets are off.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:39.872
They can read all your data.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:43.276
So it has to be done randomly
and we need to be able to keep it.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:49.182
We also need to be able to keep up with the lifecycle of a key.
That is, don't encrypt at once and forget it.

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:55.588
It's not encrypt and forget, it's encrypt
and then continuously follow the lifecycle and re-encrypt

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.090
at appropriate times.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.692
We have keys that we rotate in and we rotate out.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:03.062
Another aspect of all this
that you need to be paying attention

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:06.065
to is this notion of quantum-safe crypto.

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:12.338
Quantum computers are going to be able to break
all of our existing cryptography in the next number of years.

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:14.774
We don't know exactly when, but it's not that far off.

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:20.012
And so we're going to need to keep an eye on this space
right here so that we use new algorithms

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:26.119
that will keep our data safe against the quantum threat
that would be out there if someone had a quantum computer

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:30.556
and started trying to crack all of our data
that we had previously secured.

00:06:31.324 --> 00:06:33.760
Access control is another part of data security.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.565
Access control is also part of identity and access management,
which was the first of the seven domains that we covered.

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:45.405
But it relates here as well, because it doesn't matter
how strong I've encrypted the information.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:49.709
If a user that has access to this set their password

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:53.312
to the word "password",
then it doesn't matter because they're getting in.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:57.550
If we don't have a good way of authenticating
and authorizing users,

00:06:57.817 --> 00:07:01.487
then all of this strong crypto isn't going to matter.

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:04.757
So access controls need to be there. Backup.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:09.195
If we face in particular
a disaster recovery scenario or a ransomware

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:12.965
type scenario where someone says,
I've got your data and I'm not giving it back.

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:17.336
Well, the best defense against that is saying,
guess what, ransomware guy.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.774
I've also got a copy of my data
and I'll just restore it and you can go pound sand.

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:25.311
So this is the type of protection that we need

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:29.315
not only to keep the data from prying eyes,
but also keep the data

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:33.219
so that we have a level of resilience that I mentioned earlier

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:37.023
in case of attack or disaster or things of that sort.

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:40.860
Also, I need to be able to ensure

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.297
that I am complying to some of the industry regulations
that might exist.

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:48.434
The Generalized Data Protection Regulation in Europe, GDPR

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.671
we have in the US, HIPAA for health care information.

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:55.641
There are lots of regulations in lots of industries
and lots of parts of the world.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:58.978
It's essentially,
if you've got any information about somebody,

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:03.783
there's a chance that you have
would be subject to one of these regulatory requirements.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:09.722
So I need to be able to report on
am I complying with that? Just as much,

00:08:09.722 --> 00:08:14.961
I need to be able to report when I'm not complying
because if I don't know that, then one of the costs

00:08:14.961 --> 00:08:19.765
that goes into these data breaches are the fines
that go along with it and with GDPR in particular.

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:21.300
It's very substantial.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.137
And if you think because you're operating a company
that's not in the in Europe,

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:28.140
in the European Union, that you're exempt from this.

00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:29.108
Think again.

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:35.081
If you've got European citizen data, even if you don't
operate there, you could potentially be subject to that.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.383
Check with your lawyers to find out.

00:08:37.383 --> 00:08:39.452
And then a policy.

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:43.389
I need an ability to retain records, but only as long as

00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:46.492
is necessary according to the law or other regulation.

00:08:46.993 --> 00:08:51.464
It really doesn't do us good to keep a lot of information
and just store everything forever.

00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:56.836
So what we need to do, though, is the law will require
that we store data for a certain period of time

00:08:57.169 --> 00:09:01.240
and we want to store it for that long and probably not longer
because it gets more expensive.

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:05.611
And the longer we're holding this data,
the longer we're holding the burden

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:10.716
that if in fact, we have one of these breaches,
we could be held liable.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.420
So it's best not to have it in the first place
if you don't really need it.

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:16.022
Okay.

00:09:16.022 --> 00:09:20.626
Now, we've covered the first bunch of security protections
that are needed

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.263
about governance, about discovery and things of that sort.

00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:29.969
Now we're going to take a look at two more-- detection and
response. Security is about prevention, detection and response.

00:09:30.336 --> 00:09:32.471
These first ones are mostly about the prevention.

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:35.541
This is about the detection and response. Detection.

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:37.176
Now, what does that involve?

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:41.414
Well, I need to be able to monitor my systems
and see how data is being used.

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:46.519
How is it moving around my organization, who's using it, under
what conditions?

00:09:46.886 --> 00:09:51.090
And in fact, I also may want to use a technology
called user behavior analytics (UBA)

00:09:51.324 --> 00:09:54.894
that will monitor users
and the way that they're looking at the data,

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:58.931
if they're downloading normally a thousand files a day
and suddenly they start

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:03.069
downloading a million files in a day,
then that could be suspicious.

00:10:03.302 --> 00:10:06.405
Or if someone who normally doesn't access certain data starts

00:10:06.405 --> 00:10:10.109
having a lot of interest in that data,
then that could be an issue.

00:10:10.276 --> 00:10:14.280
If someone is doing something different than their peer group,
then that could be an issue.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.150
So that's what user behavior analytics is looking for,
those kind of cases.

00:10:18.451 --> 00:10:21.954
So we're trying to detect misuse and abuse of the data.

00:10:22.321 --> 00:10:26.325
And ultimately, I'm going to generate alerts
that are going to go up to some console

00:10:26.492 --> 00:10:29.562
and someone then can go take an action on that.

00:10:29.562 --> 00:10:31.631
And the action is our response part.

00:10:31.864 --> 00:10:34.500
So one of the things I might do is open a case

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:38.504
and then assign that case to someone
and then they begin an investigation.

00:10:38.938 --> 00:10:43.442
We might guide all of those efforts with something
called a dynamic playbook.

00:10:43.743 --> 00:10:47.079
A dynamic playbook would tell you, this is what just happened.

00:10:47.213 --> 00:10:49.115
Now you need to do the following steps.

00:10:49.115 --> 00:10:54.120
And based upon the results of those steps, I'm
going to specify other steps for you to follow.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.856
So that's the dynamic nature of it, as opposed to just a

00:10:57.156 --> 00:11:00.292
a hardcoded script that someone follows.

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:04.697
This is more like a dynamic script that they can follow. 
But it leads people through

00:11:05.297 --> 00:11:07.967
and allows them to automate the recovery

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.138
and orchestration and automation of these problems.

00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:13.773
What's the difference between those two?

00:11:13.773 --> 00:11:16.442
In a perfect world, I'd automate all of my responses.

00:11:16.442 --> 00:11:20.146
But we don't live in a perfect world
because we see a lot of these things

00:11:20.146 --> 00:11:24.183
that are first of a kind type of situations.
A first of a kind,

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:28.854
I can't automate because I don't know what I should have done
because I've never seen it before.

00:11:28.854 --> 00:11:32.692
Orchestrate is what I have to do in a lot of these cases
where basically

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:35.528
I'm looking for certain things and I'm providing guidance,

00:11:35.828 --> 00:11:41.100
but like an orchestra, the conductor of the orchestra
is conducting who's going to come in,

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.568
when do the trumpets come in,

00:11:42.568 --> 00:11:45.538
when do the the saxophones come in. This sort of thing.

00:11:45.838 --> 00:11:50.943
They're going to basically direct all of this,
and that's what we're going to do in our response.

00:11:51.377 --> 00:11:52.812
So think about those things.

00:11:52.812 --> 00:11:56.315
We're going to talk about these actually
in more detail in our next two videos

00:11:56.482 --> 00:12:00.019
where we're going to talk about security
monitoring and security response.

00:12:01.454 --> 00:12:01.987
Okay.

00:12:01.987 --> 00:12:05.191
Now we've done a quick flyover view of data security.

00:12:05.491 --> 00:12:10.663
We started off talking about governance, setting the plan
and saying this is what we intend to do.

00:12:10.963 --> 00:12:13.933
And if we don't have that right,
we can't expect to do the other parts right.

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:19.772
Then we move into discovery, find out where all the data is
that we need to apply those policies to.

00:12:20.106 --> 00:12:22.441
Then we're going to put those protections in place.

00:12:22.975 --> 00:12:27.613
Then we're going to check our compliance and see if,
in fact we're doing what we intended to do.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:29.982
We're going to look for anomalies.

00:12:29.982 --> 00:12:35.221
Then we're going to respond when we find those and feed
that information back into our policy.

00:12:35.354 --> 00:12:38.324
So the whole thing then becomes this ecosystem

00:12:38.324 --> 00:12:41.327
of data security,
and it involves a lot of different technologies.

00:12:41.727 --> 00:12:43.796
So that's in general how we want to do this.

00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:47.933
And now what I told you is if you would stay to the end,

00:12:47.933 --> 00:12:51.203
I'm going to tell you the top five things
and you can see them here already.

00:12:51.537 --> 00:12:53.506
So no need for a drum roll.

00:12:53.506 --> 00:12:57.643
But according to the cost of data breach survey,
these were the top

00:12:57.643 --> 00:13:01.280
five things that reduced the cost of a data breach.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.851
Number one,
I using artificial intelligence was top on the list.

00:13:06.152 --> 00:13:10.790
And in fact we started using
AI a good deal already in this detect phase.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:16.562
You'll hear more about that in the future videos as we as
we cover that in the next one.

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:20.833
But you can expect to see AI be infused
in all of these different spaces.

00:13:21.033 --> 00:13:23.068
So look for that moving forward.

00:13:23.502 --> 00:13:28.641
DevSecOps. That was a big part of the discussion
in the previous video on application security.

00:13:28.641 --> 00:13:31.310
If you missed that, make sure you go back and check that out.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:35.648
But that breaks down the walls
between dev[elopment], sec[urity] and ops organizations

00:13:35.848 --> 00:13:40.319
and put security in a shift left position. Incident response.

00:13:40.586 --> 00:13:41.954
We talked about that here.

00:13:41.954 --> 00:13:46.859
That's this response capability and it's going to be
the subject of our video two videos from now.

00:13:47.126 --> 00:13:50.396
So again, stay tuned for that. Cryptography.

00:13:50.396 --> 00:13:53.299
We've talked about that here in this particular space.

00:13:53.432 --> 00:13:56.402
And as I said, if you can't encrypt the data,
you can't protect it.

00:13:56.902 --> 00:13:59.271
And then ultimately employee training,

00:13:59.471 --> 00:14:02.842
because at the end of the day, it's
not all about the technology.

00:14:02.842 --> 00:14:06.078
There's an end user and the user, the human, is

00:14:06.078 --> 00:14:09.048
almost always the weakest link in any security system.

00:14:09.315 --> 00:14:11.317
So don't ignore this part.

00:14:11.317 --> 00:14:13.719
If you do, it's at your own peril.

00:14:13.719 --> 00:14:15.120
Okay. There we go.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:18.991
Those are the top five things that you can do
to reduce the cost of a data breach,

00:14:19.291 --> 00:14:22.628
to reduce the likelihood
that it ever happens to you in the first place.

00:14:22.962 --> 00:14:25.531
Make sure you have a good plan for doing all of these things.

00:14:26.332 --> 00:14:26.799
All right.

00:14:26.799 --> 00:14:29.602
Now, in the next video, we will cover

00:14:29.768 --> 00:14:33.105
monitoring and then the following video security response.

00:14:33.272 --> 00:14:37.042
So make sure you stay tuned to check those out--[click] like, subscribe,

